{{#if this.bareUserOutletLayout}}
  {{!-- BARE ROUTES bring their own shell, so they skip the `.md-shell--user` wrapper below --
       Reports (user.stats) renders `.md-shell--reports-view` instead. Reports is still an
       account-section page and still wants the nav, so the rail renders here too, and the
       gutter it needs is carried by a wrapper of its own rather than by `.md-shell--user-rail`
       (which this branch never gets).
       Gated on the SAME `accountRailContext` as the other branch, so the two can never
       disagree about which pages have a rail. Boards and the board routes are also bare but
       are not in that list, so they fall through to the plain outlet unchanged. --}}
  {{!-- Chrome (rail + pill nav) is mounted ONCE in templates/application.hbs as of
         2026-09-21, so it persists across transitions instead of being rebuilt here. --}}
  {{outlet}}
{{else}}
<div class="md-user-layout hide_print">
  <div class="md-shell md-shell--user">
    {{!-- BASIC VIEW'S SECTION NAV (2026-09-25, requested). Modern's rail is mounted in the app
         shell; Basic has none, so it is rendered here.
         A DIRECT CHILD OF `.md-shell`, following templates/organizations.hbs, and for the same
         two reasons given at that call site: `.md-shell` carries the page's background, so a
         rail outside it would sit on bare page while the gradient covered only the content
         column; and `.md-shell > .md-workspace` is a DIRECT-CHILD selector, so a flex wrapper
         between the two would silently stop it applying and change Modern's layout for a
         Basic-only feature. `.md-shell` is already `display: flex`; Basic only turns it to a
         row (_classic-home.scss), and Modern keeps the column, untouched. --}}
    {{#if this.showClassicAccountRail}}
      <Dashboard::ClassicAccountRail @user={{this.model}} />
    {{/if}}
    {{!-- THE ACCOUNT SECTION NAV (2026-09-18). One instance here serves every page in the
         section: it used to live inside templates/user/index.hbs, so only the account page
         had it. `accountRailContext` (controllers/user.js) decides WHICH routes get it, and
         `md-shell--user-rail` above reserves the 208px the fixed panel occupies -- the two
         are driven by the same flag, so a page can never get the panel without the gutter or
         the gutter without the panel. --}}
    {{!-- `md-workspace--homenav` is gone (2026-09-21). It marked the logs page reached from
         the Updates pill, for 64px of extra headroom that cleared a nav which no longer
         renders inside the page -- the app shell clears the hoisted nav for every
         destination now. See the note left at that rule's old site in app.scss. --}}
    <div class="md-workspace">
      {{!-- WHICH NAV, DECIDED BY WHERE THE USER CAME FROM (requested 2026-09-14).
           `homeNavContext` (controllers/user.js) is true only on the logs page and only when it
           was reached from the home page's Updates pill, which says so with `nav=home` in the
           URL. In that one case the HOME menu stays on screen with Updates active, so the user
           keeps the menu they were navigating with. Every other page in the section gets the
           account rail, mounted above.

           THIS WAS A THREE-WAY BRANCH until 2026-09-21: `accountRailContext` first (rendering
           nothing, because the rail is mounted above), `homeNavContext` second, and a final
           else carrying the `.md-pillnav--user` account pill row plus its collapsed drawer.

           THAT PILL ROW IS RETIRED, and the rail is what replaced it. The rail carries all five
           of its links -- Home, Account, Profile, Settings, Logs -- with the same
           `permissions.edit` gate on Profile and Settings, applied to the same record (both were
           handed `this.model`). The rail's Logs row also drops the pill's `?type=all`, which was
           not free: controllers/user/logs.js normalises 'all' to null, that write is observed by
           `refresh_on_params_change`, and routes/user/logs.js has already called `refresh` in
           setupController -- so arriving with the param cost a SECOND store.query('log') on one
           of the heavier endpoints in the app.

           COLLAPSED TO A SINGLE IF rather than left as an empty else: `accountRailContext`
           returns false whenever `homeNavContext` is true (controllers/user.js), so the first
           branch could never preempt the second, and with the third deleted both remaining
           non-home branches rendered nothing at all. This is exactly equivalent output.

           THE PAGES THAT USED TO REACH THE DELETED BRANCH now get the rail instead -- see the
           six route names added to `accountRailContext`, and the matching ROW_FOR_ROUTE keys in
           components/account-rail.js, which have to move together.

           `user.password_reset` and `user.confirm_registration` deliberately get NO nav: they
           are single-task pages reached from an email, they carry their own exit, and the
           global header in application.hbs renders for signed-out visitors too. --}}
      {{!-- `md-main--updates-arrival` marks the logs page reached from the home pill-nav's
           Updates, so its header can centre there (app.scss). Driven by the same
           `homeNavContext` the nav choice uses, so the two can never disagree about which
           arrival this is.
           A NEW NAME, not the retired `md-workspace--homenav`: that one existed for headroom
           that a since-removed in-page nav needed, and reviving it would imply this is the
           same concern. It is not — this is purely the header's alignment. --}}
      <main class="md-main md-main--user {{if this.homeNavContext "md-main--updates-arrival"}}">
        {{outlet}}
      </main>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
{{/if}}
